<!-- §01 THE KV CACHE PROBLEM -->
<section id="s-problem" data-code="problem">
<div class="section-no">01 — The KV Cache Problem</div>
<h2>Why Transformers Can't Forget</h2>
<p>Every time a transformer generates the next token, it computes attention — a weighted sum of all past tokens' value vectors, where the weights are determined by how much each past key vector aligns with the current query. To avoid recomputing everything from scratch, the key-value pairs for all past tokens are cached in memory: the KV cache.</p>
<p>The memory footprint scales linearly with context length. For a model with 32 layers, 8 attention heads, and key/value dimension 128, storing a single token costs 32 × 8 × 2 × 128 = 65,536 floats. At 16-bit precision, that's 128 KB <em>per token</em>. A 100K-token context needs 12.5 GB — just for the cache.</p>

<div class="vp">
  <div class="vp-head">
    <span class="vp-title">kv-cache-growth.canvas — memory vs context length</span>
    <span class="vp-hint">drag the slider to explore</span>
  </div>
  <div class="vp-body" style="padding-bottom:.5rem">
    <canvas id="kv-canvas" height="200"></canvas>
    <div style="display:flex;align-items:center;gap:1rem;margin-top:.875rem;flex-wrap:wrap">
      <label style="font-size:.72rem;color:var(--muted);font-family:var(--ff-mono)">Context length:
        <input type="range" id="ctx-slider" min="1" max="128" value="32"
          style="margin-left:.5rem;vertical-align:middle;accent-color:var(--purple);width:140px">
        <span id="ctx-val" style="color:var(--text2);margin-left:.4rem">32K tokens</span>
      </label>
      <div style="font-size:.72rem;color:var(--muted);font-family:var(--ff-mono)">
        Cache: <span id="cache-gb" style="color:var(--purple-l);font-weight:700">4.0 GB</span>
        &nbsp;·&nbsp; Full KV: <span id="full-gb" style="color:rgba(239,68,68,.8);font-weight:700">4.0 GB</span>
        &nbsp;·&nbsp; Approx (ε=0.01): <span id="approx-gb" style="color:var(--green);font-weight:700">— MB</span>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

<p>The research question: can we compute accurate attention outputs while storing only a <em>compressed sketch</em> of past tokens — a small subset or summary that answers any future query within a guaranteed error bound ε?</p>
</section>

<!-- §02 STREAMING APPROXIMATION -->
<section id="s-formulation" data-code="formulation">
<div class="section-no">02 — Streaming Approximation</div>
<h2>The Problem, Precisely</h2>
<p>The attention function takes keys k<sub>1</sub>,…,k<sub>n</sub>, values v<sub>1</sub>,…,v<sub>n</sub>, and query q, and returns:</p>

<div class="math-block">
  <div class="math-label">Attention Formula</div>
  Attn(K, q, V) = <span class="math-var">Σ</span> [exp(⟨k<sub>i</sub>, q⟩) / <span class="math-var">Σ</span> exp(⟨k<sub>j</sub>, q⟩)] · v<sub>i</sub>
  <br><span class="math-comment">// weighted average of values; weights are softmax of key-query dot products</span>
</div>

<p>In the <strong>streaming model</strong>, tokens arrive one by one. After each new token, the algorithm must be able to answer any query q with guaranteed accuracy. Crucially, it can only use <em>limited memory</em> — it cannot store all n key-value pairs. The goal is to maintain a compact data structure that approximates attention to within ε relative error.</p>

<p>The key difficulty comes from the softmax kernel: exp(⟨k, q⟩) = e<sup>⟨k,q⟩</sup>. The exponential makes it very sensitive to alignment between keys and queries. A key k that aligns perfectly with q gets exponentially more weight than one that doesn't — this is what makes attention expressive, and also what makes approximating it tricky.</p>

<div class="callout">
  <div class="callout-label">The Formal Problem</div>
  <p>Maintain a data structure using <em>S</em> bits that, for any new query q, outputs ẑ such that ‖ẑ − Attn(K, q, V)‖ ≤ ε · ‖softmax(K, q)‖ · ‖V‖<sub>F</sub>. The vectors k, q are bounded: ‖k‖, ‖q‖ ≤ r. What is the minimum S?</p>
</div>

<p>Two parameters control the difficulty: the precision ε (smaller = harder) and the radius r (larger = more "peaked" softmax, harder to approximate). This paper gives nearly matching upper and lower bounds in terms of both.</p>
</section>

<!-- §03 THE OPEN GAP -->
<section id="s-gap" data-code="gap">
<div class="section-no">03 — The Open Gap</div>
<h2>A Puzzling Mismatch</h2>
<p>Prior to this work, the state of the art left a wide gap. The best known algorithm (Kochetkova et al., NeurIPS 2025) used a discrepancy-based coreset and achieved space complexity O(√d · e<sup>2r²</sup> / ε). Their matching lower bound, however, didn't depend on ε at all — only on r.</p>

<div class="bound-compare">
  <div class="bound-card old">
    <div class="bound-card-label">Prior upper bound (KSH+ 2025)</div>
    <div class="bound-formula">Õ( √d · e<sup>2r²</sup> / ε )</div>
    <div class="bound-note">Linear in 1/ε — improves with lower precision</div>
  </div>
  <div class="bound-card old">
    <div class="bound-card-label">Prior lower bound (KSH+ 2025)</div>
    <div class="bound-formula">Ω̃( min(n, e<sup>2r²</sup>) )</div>
    <div class="bound-note">No ε dependence — the bound doesn't get stronger as ε decreases!</div>
  </div>
</div>

<p>This gap is enormous. The algorithm says "halve ε → double the memory." The lower bound says "you might not need any extra memory at all." One of them had to be wrong, or at least far from tight. Which one?</p>

<p>The answer turns out to be: <strong>both</strong> were loose, in different regimes. The true memory requirement is a sublinear function of 1/ε — the correct exponent depends on the dimension d. This paper finds it.</p>

