<!-- §04 THE ALGORITHM -->
<section id="s-algorithm" data-code="algorithm">
<div class="section-no">04 — The Algorithm</div>
<h2>Split, Sketch, and Combine</h2>
<p>The key insight is to decompose the exponential kernel using its Taylor series, then handle the two parts separately. Recall that e<sup>x</sup> = Σ x<sup>l</sup>/l!. For a cutoff t, write:</p>

<div class="math-block">
  <div class="math-label">Taylor Split</div>
  exp(K, q) = <span class="math-var">Σ<sub>k∈K</sub></span> [exp<sub>≤t</sub>(k, q) + exp<sub>&gt;t</sub>(k, q)]
  <br>
  exp<sub>≤t</sub>(k, q) = <span class="math-var">Σ<sub>l=0</sub><sup>t</sup></span> ⟨k, q⟩<sup>l</sup> / l! &nbsp;&nbsp;<span class="math-comment">// low-degree part</span>
  <br>
  exp<sub>&gt;t</sub>(k, q) = <span class="math-var">Σ<sub>l=t+1</sub><sup>∞</sup></span> ⟨k, q⟩<sup>l</sup> / l! &nbsp;&nbsp;<span class="math-comment">// high-degree part</span>
</div>

<p>This split creates two subproblems, each solvable with a different technique:</p>

<div class="split-diagram">
  <div class="split-box">
    <div class="split-box-title" style="color:var(--cyan)">📐 Low-Degree Moments</div>
    <div class="split-box-body">Store the aggregate <strong>polynomial sketch</strong> Σ<sub>k∈K</sub> ψ₁(k), where ψ₁(k) = (k⊗l/l!)<sub>l=0..t</sub> stores all moments of keys up to degree t. Query evaluation is just a dot product. Space: O((d+t)ᵗ/t!) — grows with t but doesn't depend on n.</div>
  </div>
  <div class="split-plus">+</div>
  <div class="split-box">
    <div class="split-box-title" style="color:var(--purple)">🎯 High-Degree Coreset</div>
    <div class="split-box-body">Store a small <strong>weighted subset</strong> C ⊂ K that approximates the high-degree kernel. Because exp<sub>&gt;t</sub> is positive definite, Banaszczyk's theorem (from discrepancy theory) guarantees such a subset exists. Size: O(er²/ε · Σ<sub>l&gt;t</sub> r²ˡ/l!) — shrinks as t grows.</div>
  </div>
</div>

<p>The trick is to choose <em>t</em> to balance these two costs. As t increases, the sketch grows but the coreset shrinks. The optimal t minimizes their sum — and it turns out to be t ≈ log(1/ε) / log(d), giving a total space of (1/ε)<sup>1 − 1/a + o(1)</sup> when d = log<sup>a</sup>n.</p>

<div class="vp">
  <div class="vp-head">
    <span class="vp-title">algorithm-split.canvas — sketch vs coreset cost as t varies</span>
    <span class="vp-hint">drag t to find the optimal split</span>
  </div>
  <div class="vp-body" style="padding-bottom:.5rem">
    <canvas id="split-canvas" height="210"></canvas>
