        Sketch: <span id="sketch-cost" style="color:var(--cyan);font-weight:700">—</span>
        &nbsp;·&nbsp; Coreset: <span id="coreset-cost" style="color:var(--purple-l);font-weight:700">—</span>
        &nbsp;·&nbsp; Total: <span id="total-cost" style="color:var(--text2);font-weight:700">—</span>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

<h3>The Step-by-Step Algorithm</h3>
<div class="step-list" id="step-list">
  <div class="step-box active">
    <div class="step-num">1</div>
    <div class="step-body">
      <div class="step-title">Choose cutoff t</div>
      <div class="step-desc">Set t = ⌊log(1/ε) / log(d)⌋. This balances the low-degree sketch size against the coreset size.</div>
      <div class="step-code">t = floor(log(1/eps) / log(d))</div>
    </div>
  </div>
  <div class="step-box">
    <div class="step-num">2</div>
    <div class="step-body">
      <div class="step-title">Maintain polynomial sketch</div>
      <div class="step-desc">For each incoming key k, accumulate ψ₁(k) = (k⊗l / l!)ₗ₌₀..t into the running sum. Store O((d+t)ᵗ/t!) numbers.</div>
      <div class="step-code">sketch += outer_powers(k, t)  # O(d^t / t!) floats</div>
    </div>
  </div>
  <div class="step-box">
    <div class="step-num">3</div>
    <div class="step-body">
      <div class="step-title">Maintain coreset for high-degree part</div>
      <div class="step-desc">Run a discrepancy-minimization oracle (e.g., the randomized Gram-Schmidt walk) to maintain a weighted subset C ⊆ K for exp<sub>&gt;t</sub>. Double the size when needed, halve using BaseCompress.</div>
      <div class="step-code">C = base_compress(K, exp_gt(t), eps)  # |C| = O(e^r2/eps)</div>
    </div>
  </div>
  <div class="step-box">
    <div class="step-num">4</div>
    <div class="step-body">
      <div class="step-title">Answer queries</div>
      <div class="step-desc">For query q, compute the low-degree part from the sketch and the high-degree part from the coreset; sum for the full approximation.</div>
      <div class="step-code">z_low  = dot(sketch, outer_powers(q, t))
z_high = sum(w_k * exp_gt(k,q) for k,w in C)
return z_low + z_high</div>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>
<div class="step-nav">
  <button id="step-prev" disabled>← Prev</button>
  <span class="step-prog" id="step-prog">Step 1 of 4</span>
  <button id="step-next">Next →</button>
</div>
</section>

<!-- §05 TEMPERATURE REGIMES -->
<section id="s-temperature" data-code="temperature">
<div class="section-no">05 — Temperature Regimes</div>
<h2>Hot vs. Cold Attention</h2>
<p>The radius r controls how "sharp" the attention distribution can be. Think of r as an inverse temperature: when r is large, the softmax can concentrate nearly all its weight on one token; when r is small, the weights stay roughly uniform.</p>

<div class="vp">
  <div class="vp-head">
    <span class="vp-title">temperature.canvas — softmax sharpness for r = 1, 2, 4</span>
    <span class="vp-hint">click a row to highlight</span>
  </div>
  <div class="vp-body">
    <canvas id="temp-canvas" height="230"></canvas>
    <div style="display:flex;gap:.625rem;margin-top:.875rem;flex-wrap:wrap">
      <button class="temp-btn active" data-r="1" style="padding:.25rem .7rem;font-size:.68rem;font-family:var(--ff-mono);border:1px solid var(--border-hi);border-radius:4px;background:var(--cyan-dim);color:var(--cyan);cursor:pointer">r = 1 (high temp)</button>
      <button class="temp-btn" data-r="2" style="padding:.25rem .7rem;font-size:.68rem;font-family:var(--ff-mono);border:1px solid var(--border-hi);border-radius:4px;background:var(--surface);color:var(--muted2);cursor:pointer">r = 2</button>
      <button class="temp-btn" data-r="3" style="padding:.25rem .7rem;font-size:.68rem;font-family:var(--ff-mono);border:1px solid var(--border-hi);border-radius:4px;background:var(--surface);color:var(--muted2);cursor:pointer">r = 3 (low temp)</button>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

<p>The two regimes require different algorithmic strategies:</p>

<ul>
  <li><strong>High temperature (small r):</strong> The softmax is spread out. The polynomial sketch approach dominates — you need relatively few terms in the Taylor expansion to approximate the smooth distribution. The memory bound is (1/ε)<sup>1−1/a</sup>, where a comes from d = log<sup>a</sup>n.</li>
  <li><strong>Low temperature (large r, r² = Ω(log n)):</strong> Attention concentrates sharply. The algorithm must find and exploit cluster structure in the key set — "recentering and rescaling" dense clusters to reduce the effective radius, then applying the base coreset construction to each cluster independently. The memory bound is e<sup>r²(1±o(1)</sup>/ε.</li>
</ul>

<div class="callout callout-cyan">
  <div class="callout-label">Key insight: recentering saves exponential factors</div>
  <p>If all keys cluster near center c, running a coreset over the raw keys wastes exp(2r²) space. But if you first shift to K−c and rescale, the effective radius drops from r to √(r·r'), saving the difference exponentially. The paper shows this recentering step is what achieves the nearly tight e<sup>r²(1±o(1))</sup> bound in the low-temperature regime.</p>
</div>
</section>

<!-- §06 TIGHT BOUNDS -->
<section id="s-results" data-code="results">
<div class="section-no">06 — Tight Bounds</div>
<h2>The Final Scorecard</h2>
<p>The main result is two theorems — one for each regime — each giving nearly matching upper and lower bounds.</p>

<div class="bound-compare">
  <div class="bound-card new">
    <div class="bound-card-label">High-Temperature Regime (Theorem 2.1)</div>
    <div class="bound-formula">
      Upper: Õ( (1/ε)<sup>1 − 1/a + o(1)</sup> )<br>
      Lower: Ω̃( (1/ε)<sup>1 − 1/a − o(1)</sup> )
    </div>
    <div class="bound-note">For d = log<sup>a</sup>n, r = (log n)<sup>o(1)</sup>. Tight up to subpolynomial factors for integer a &gt; 2.</div>
  </div>
  <div class="bound-card new">
    <div class="bound-card-label">Low-Temperature Regime (Theorem 2.2)</div>
    <div class="bound-formula">
      Upper: Õ( e<sup>r²(1+o(1))</sup> / ε )<br>
      Lower: Ω̃( min(n, e<sup>r²(1−o(1))</sup> / ε) )
    </div>
    <div class="bound-note">For r² = Ω(log n), d = (log n)<sup>1+Ω(1)</sup>. Nearly tight in the exponent of e<sup>r²</sup>.</div>
  </div>
</div>

<h3>The improvement over prior work</h3>

<div class="results-bars" id="bounds-bars">
  <div class="bar-section-head">Space needed for ε = 0.001 (d = log²n, r = 1)</div>
  <div class="bar-row">
    <div class="bar-label">Prior upper bound<br><small style="font-size:.62rem;color:var(--muted)">(KSH+25) O(1/ε)</small></div>
    <div class="bar-track"><div class="bar-fill" data-w="100" style="background:rgba(239,68,68,.5)"><span>1000 units</span></div></div>
  </div>
  <div class="bar-row">
    <div class="bar-label">This paper's upper bound<br><small style="font-size:.62rem;color:var(--muted)">(1/ε)<sup>1−1/2</sup> = (1/ε)<sup>0.5</sup></small></div>
    <div class="bar-track"><div class="bar-fill" data-w="31.6" style="background:var(--purple)"><span>≈ 31.6 units</span></div></div>
  </div>
  <div class="bar-row">
    <div class="bar-label">Prior lower bound<br><small style="font-size:.62rem;color:var(--muted)">(KSH+25) Ω(1) — no ε dep.</small></div>
    <div class="bar-track"><div class="bar-fill" data-w="1" style="background:rgba(107,114,128,.4)"><span>1 unit</span></div></div>
  </div>
  <div class="bar-row">
    <div class="bar-label">This paper's lower bound<br><small style="font-size:.62rem;color:var(--muted)">(1/ε)<sup>1−1/2−o(1)</sup> ≈ 28 units</small></div>
    <div class="bar-track"><div class="bar-fill" data-w="28" style="background:var(--cyan)"><span>≈ 28 units</span></div></div>
  </div>
</div>

<p>The gap between the upper and lower bounds drops from a factor of 1000× (old) to just ≈ 1.1× (new) — nearly tight. The prior 1/ε upper bound was off by a factor of √(1/ε) = √1000 ≈ 31 at ε = 0.001.</p>

<h3>Why the lower bound is hard</h3>
<p>Proving that <em>no</em> algorithm can do better requires a carefully designed lower bound. The paper reduces streaming attention to the classic <strong>INDEX communication problem</strong>: Alice holds a binary string x of length N, Bob holds index i; Alice sends one message, Bob must output x<sub>i</sub>. This requires Ω(N) bits.</p>
<p>The reduction maps each bit x<sub>j</sub> to the presence or absence of a key k<sub>j</sub> in the input stream. Bob queries the attention algorithm at q = k<sub>i</sub> and uses the output to infer x<sub>i</sub>. The main challenge: the cross-terms (keys j ≠ i) create noise that drowns the signal. The paper's key novelty is sending "side information" — a degree-t sketch of the input — alongside the algorithm state, and using the <strong>Hermite basis</strong> (rather than Taylor monomials) to make the signal-to-noise ratio work out exactly right.</p>

<div class="callout">
  <div class="callout-label">The matching magic</div>
  <p>The upper and lower bounds match because the same expression — min<sub>t</sub>[ (d+t)ᵗ/t! + (er²/ε)·Σ<sub>l&gt;t</sub> r²ˡ/l! ] — appears in both. The algorithm minimizes it to choose t; the lower bound shows you can't do better than the minimum. The Hermite expansion is precisely calibrated to make this match exact.</p>
</div>
</section>

